  "body": "M12.1 stage 4 (#916): a STEP importer on the solidcore side of the kernel seam, and the dual-importer bench against truck-stepio. **Bench only** - `crates/app` still imports STEP through truck; truck and ruststep stay. Everything is inside `crates/kernel`; no other workspace crate gains a dependency.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Pin.** `crates/kernel` takes `solidcore`, `solidcore-step` and `solidcore-convert` from `https://github.com/NeoTech/solidcore` at `rev = \"55d0e7638b9e1ebbf91ab8c10657e595c114ecda\"` (the fork's tag `camcraft-pin-2026-11-11`) in place of crates.io `solidcore = \"=1.0.1\"`. `solidcore-ops` and `solidcore-record` come with them; `solidcore-tess` at the same rev is a dev-dependency. `Cargo.lock`: 5 git packages replace the 1 registry package (552 -> 556), no new crates.io package. The lock diff touches only those packages and camcraft-kernel's own dependency list.\n- **Against 1.0.1** (the pin is 2973 commits past the `solidcore-v1.0.1` tag; the seam's library code compiled unchanged): `Brep` is no longer `Clone` (two test helpers now copy with `brep_copy` or measure in place); the port now refuses a NaN offset distance; the native reader now refuses blank, foreign, noise and header-only files itself. Five pinned port verdicts re-triaged - all were findings against the port, now closed by it.\n- **Importer** (`camcraft_kernel::solidcore_step`): `read_step` (every solid item, refused ones with stage and reason, the file's length units, notes) and `import_step` (a model only when every solid item came through). 64 MiB size bound; the caller's `CancelToken` is the time bound (new `KernelError::TimedOut`), polled by the parser, between solids and inside the kernel's loops; panics caught. The stage-3 laws apply unchanged (integrity, closed manifold solid, vertex gap <= 1e-3 mm). A length unit the reader cannot convert is refused by the seam (the reader alone reads it as millimetres, silently).\n- **Bench** (`tests/step_agreement.rs`): every `.step` under `testdata/models` through both importers; verdicts pinned; a fixture with no row fails.\n- **Corrupt corpus** (`tests/step_import.rs`): 19 files made from box.step through both importers, each under a 60 s bound that cancels the import.\n\n## Readings\n\n| File | Unit (mm) | Importers | solidcore-tess mesh |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| box.step, empty_list.step (#887), tool_csys_box_iso13399.step | 1 | agree | watertight, within tolerance |\n| sample_ap214.step (metre, 3 solids) | 1000 | agree | watertight, within tolerance |\n| Milling-test.step, Milling-test-inch.step | 1 / 25.4 | **disagree: bounding box** | watertight, within tolerance |\n| tool_csys_none / _iso13399 / _degenerate (no solid) | 1 | both refuse by name | - |\n\nThe disagreement: the port's \"tight\" box of Milling-test is Y -30..30; the part is a disc with a flat at Y = 19.719 (file vertices, truck's mesh and solidcore's own mesh all agree). Volume and area agree to 1.2e-4. solidcore is the wrong side - filed as #939.\n\nCorrupt corpus: no panic, no hang. solidcore refuses 17 of 19 by name and imports 2 that are whole. truck-stepio, as the bench reads it, refuses the 10 that break the Part 21 syntax and answers `Ok` for the other 9, five of them with an open mesh.\n\nTessellation (point 4): `solidcore-tess` at the app's 0.005 mm chord has 0 open triangle sides on every fixture (truck: 854 on Milling-test, 3196 on sample_ap214) and is closer to the exact volume (5.6e-5 against 1.2e-4 relative on Milling-test), with more triangles (6228 against 952). What a machining mesh still owes is #940.\n\nwasm32 (point 5): `cargo check -p camcraft-kernel --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` passes.\n\n## How tested\n\nRun in release, `cargo test --release -p camcraft-kernel --no-fail-fast`: 25 passed, 0 failed, 2 ignored (heavy lane); the 2 heavy-lane tests also pass with `--ignored`. `cargo clippy --release -p camcraft-kernel --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\nPinning tests (new): `step_files_agree_or_refuse_by_name`, `importer_hands_out_closed_forms_and_units`, `corrupt_step_files_never_panic_or_hang`, `importer_keeps_its_size_and_time_bounds`. Re-pinned: `port_degenerate_inputs_never_panic_and_match_pinned_verdicts`, `corrupt_native_files_never_panic_or_hang`.\n\n## What was not exercised\n\n- **`cargo xtask gate quick` was not run.** Its plan (`--dry-run`) falls back to the batch tier because `Cargo.lock` changed: every debug suite of app, ui, motion, sim and the rest - and no kernel suite. The kernel suites and clippy above were run instead (the work-issue skill's alternative). The batch tier on this branch is owed before it merges.\n- The largest mesh-to-surface distance is not measured; the mesh is judged on volume, area, box and watertightness (#940).\n- No wasm32 test run, only the check; `solidcore-tess` was not checked for wasm32.\n- No STEP file larger than 100 KB and no third-party corpus: the fixtures are the repo's nine.\n- The time bound cannot stop the reader's topology pass (no hook upstream, #939); it is bounded by caps.\n\nFollow-ups filed: #939 (bounding box and two smaller port findings), #940 (machining mesh). Both gate #917.\n\nDecisions: `docs/decisions/916-solidcore-step-bench.md`. Notices: `assets/ui/LICENSES.md`.\n\nChangelog: The kernel seam can import STEP through solidcore's reader (bench only; the app still imports through truck), with a pinned agreement bench against truck over every STEP fixture; solidcore moves from crates.io 1.0.1 to a pinned commit of the owner's fork.\n\nEffort: max (not escalated)\n\nFixes #916\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
